{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791051923,"nonce":"JBJogkZ9+dNUfvekEf20uiqRZvM=","message":"What lazy-loading LLVM would buy, measured with an LLVM-free build (ash --no-default-features, NUC, idle RSS; with-LLVM 196ee65 in brackets): interp 10.5 MB (15.6), jit 15.8 (23.0), hybrid 11.8 (19.1). So LLVM is 5-7 MB of the idle footprint, plus hybrid's compiler heap once it compiles.\n\nThe LLVM-free 10.5 MB: executable pages ~5.0 MB (Rust .text 2.8 -- the ordering file's Rust names do not match a build with other features, so this is unordered -- .rodata 1.1, relro 0.5), anonymous 3.1 MB (about 1.0 in the GC heap reservation, 1.2 in jemalloc's first extent, its 2 MB arena_emap_global in .bss partly touched), libc/libm/ld ~1.9 MB shared. Stock hl idles at 4 MB including its libc.\n\nNot orderable: Rust's .rodata is mostly anonymous constants with no symbol for --symbol-ordering-file, and the resident 2.7 MB of it is spread over ~170 fault-around clusters with no single large table (largest resident: IntrinsicNameTableStorage 40 KB of 457, ash_std::unicase::LOWER 24 KB).","files":null}]}